        "accordion_UKWxHR": {
          "type": "accordion",
          "name": "t:names.accordion",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "plus",
            "container_size": "small",
            "show_borders": false,
            "use_product_faq_metafield": true,
            "row_color_scheme": "scheme-0c2ea821-4e44-4805-9146-bbdd95689162",
            "open_row_color_scheme": "scheme-5",
            "row_border_radius": 4,
            "row_gap": 20,
            "type_preset": "h6",
            "inherit_color_scheme": true,
            "color_scheme": "",
            "border": "none",
            "border_width": 1,
            "border_opacity": 100,
            "border_radius": 0,
            "padding-block-start": 0,
            "padding-block-end": 0,
            "padding-inline-start": 0,
            "padding-inline-end": 0
          },
          "blocks": {
            "accordion_row_6FYFfD": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "name": "t:names.accordion_row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What is the difference between cooking wood and firewood?",
                "content": "",
                "open_by_default": true,
                "icon": "none",
                "width": 20
              },
              "blocks": {
                "text_pDcgJL": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>Cooking wood is naturally<br/>aged then heat-treated and cut into smaller splits made for smokers, grills, and pizza ovens,<br/>where flavor and steady smoke matter. Firewood is meant for heat, so it is sized for fireplaces,<br/>wood stoves, and fire pits. ButlerWood sells both, and we prepare each for its job.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte",
                    "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                    "font_size": "1rem",
                    "weight": "",
                    "weight_active": "inherit",
                    "line_height": "normal",
                    "letter_spacing": "normal",
                    "case": "none",
                    "wrap": "pretty",
                    "color": "var(--color-foreground)",
                    "background": false,
                    "background_color": "#00000026",
                    "text_color": "#000000",
                    "active_color": "#d23d00",
                    "corner_radius": 0,
                    "padding-block-start": 0,
                    "padding-block-end": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end": 0,
                    "padding-block-start-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-block-end-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end-mobile": 0
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "text_pDcgJL"
              ]
            },
            "accordion_row_bApzTc": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "name": "t:names.accordion_row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "Where does ButlerWood source its wood?",
                "content": "",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none",
                "width": 20
              },
              "blocks": {
                "text_7diGN9": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>We source all of our own wood through our<br/>in-house land clearing services in Central Texas, then prepare it ourselves with our own trucking<br/>and logistics. That vertical setup is why our splits stay consistent and our prices stay lower than<br/>resellers who buy their wood from suppliers like us.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte",
                    "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                    "font_size": "1rem",
                    "weight": "",
                    "weight_active": "inherit",
                    "line_height": "normal",
                    "letter_spacing": "normal",
                    "case": "none",
                    "wrap": "pretty",
                    "color": "var(--color-foreground)",
                    "background": false,
                    "background_color": "#00000026",
                    "text_color": "#000000",
                    "active_color": "#d23d00",
                    "corner_radius": 0,
                    "padding-block-start": 0,
                    "padding-block-end": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end": 0,
                    "padding-block-start-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-block-end-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end-mobile": 0
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "text_7diGN9"
              ]
            },
            "accordion_row_i9KKCC": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "name": "t:names.accordion_row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What is the best firewood for a fireplace or fire pit?",
                "content": "",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none",
                "width": 20
              },
              "blocks": {
                "text_WFzwhp": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>For a fireplace, wood stove, or fire pit,<br/>you want a dense hardwood with low moisture content. Well-seasoned wood burns hotter, lasts<br/>longer, and produces far less creosote buildup than wet or green wood. Burning unseasoned<br/>wood wastes heat and coats your chimney. Our seasoned firewood is sold by the box, cord, and<br/>face cord so you can match the heat output you need by the cubic foot.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte",
                    "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                    "font_size": "1rem",
                    "weight": "",
                    "weight_active": "inherit",
                    "line_height": "normal",
                    "letter_spacing": "normal",
                    "case": "none",
                    "wrap": "pretty",
                    "color": "var(--color-foreground)",
                    "background": false,
                    "background_color": "#00000026",
                    "text_color": "#000000",
                    "active_color": "#d23d00",
                    "corner_radius": 0,
                    "padding-block-start": 0,
                    "padding-block-end": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end": 0,
                    "padding-block-start-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-block-end-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start-mobile": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end-mobile": 0
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "text_WFzwhp"
              ]
            },
            "accordion_row_DgLcVw": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "name": "t:names.accordion_row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "Where can I buy firewood in Texas, and do you offer pickup or shipping?",
                "content": "",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none",
                "width": 20
              },
              "blocks": {
                "text_7ckH9K": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>You can buy<br/>firewood and cooking wood online and have it shipped anywhere in the United States, with<br/>international shipping available for commercial and consumer orders. Local customers can pick<br/>up at our Seguin or Gonzales yards. Order online any time or call 830-205-1035.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
